SRC/patch -- one change made for this collection================================================inp.c and util.c (2026-09-18): UNIX FILE MODES ARE NOT OS-9 FILE MODES.WHAT WAS WRONG.  Unix `open(name, 0)' means O_RDONLY.  On OS-9, mode 0 isNO ACCESS BITS AT ALL: the open succeeds, and the I$Read after it is refusedwith E$BMode.  patch did that in five places and `creat(name, 0666)' in fourmore, where 0666 arrives as I$Create mode $B6 -- the low byte of 0x1B6.What a reader SAW was patch recognising the diff, announcing "using PlanB...", and stopping with "Error reading tmp file", leaving the target fileuntouched.  That was written down here as a defect of the program and, fora while, suspected of the emulator.  It was neither: it is the port.TRACED 2026-09-18 by the os9exec session, on a scratch clone rather thananybody's working image: `I$Open D0.b=$00 "/dd/tmp/patchia00003"' followedby `I$Read ... E_BMODE'.  The same mode-0 open of the SOURCE file is why itabandoned Plan A for Plan B in the first place.  os9link.c, in this sametree, already had it right -- S_IREAD, and S_IWRITE|S_IREAD to create --which is the tell that these two files had simply not been looked at.THE CHANGE.  Both files gain a guarded block that defines PATCH_RDONLY andPATCH_CREATMODE -- S_IREAD and S_IWRITE|S_IREAD under OSK, 0 and 0666elsewhere -- and every open() and creat() uses them.  Nothing else istouched; the Unix build is unchanged.MEASURED after the rebuild: `patch t.c < t.diff' answers "Patching file ...using Plan A... / Hunk #1 succeeded at 3. / done", the file carries the newline, and t.c.orig is beside it.  tools/datatests/dev.cases asserts that,including `absent' lines for "using Plan B..." and "Error reading tmp file"-- without those the case passes on the OLD binary, which is what the caseused to pin.The E$Share this program takes on its closing deletes is correct behaviourand not a fault: it removes temporary files it still holds open.